Closes the "real-VPS CI gate" architecture-scale item. The docker
molecule full-stack scenario covers ordering + interactions; this
workflow covers everything docker can't: real cloud-init, real
provider firewall ordering, real systemd unit startup against an
actual Debian image, plus the destroy half of the lifecycle.

Triggers (intentionally restrictive — burns provider credit, takes
~15-20 min per run):

  * workflow_dispatch with optional `zone` input
  * pull_request labeled `ci-real-deploy` (maintainer-added)

The job refuses to run on fork PRs (secrets aren't exposed there)
and a single `real-vps-deploy` concurrency group prevents two runs
from racing against the UpCloud sub-account.

Required GitHub secrets:

  UPCLOUD_USERNAME / UPCLOUD_PASSWORD   sub-account with VPS create
  CI_SOPS_AGE_KEY                       age key for CI-only secrets
                                        (separate from operator key)
  CI_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY                    one-shot SSH key

The workflow uses a per-run env name (ci-<run_id>-<timestamp>) so no
two CI deploys ever touch the same terraform state. Destroy runs in
always() so a half-built VPS never outlives the job. tfvars files
are cleaned up regardless of destroy outcome.

When secrets/ci.secrets.sops.yaml isn't committed yet, the workflow
emits a warning and skips the deploy phase, still exercising the
provisioning + teardown wiring. That blob is a planned follow-up;
committing it requires generating synthetic CI-only values and
encrypting to CI_SOPS_AGE_KEY only.

docs/CI-REAL-DEPLOY.md walks the operator through:
  * the trigger model and concurrency invariants
  * required secrets + the storage-template UUID gap
  * the cleanup invariants
  * what this gate does NOT test (long-running drift / burn /
    real users), which stays in operator-driven cadence

